## Direction C: deep/nonlinear encoders, unbalanced initialisation, timing

### C0. λ-JEPA and our balance identity

**Demirel, Dominé, Maiorca, Fumero, Mondelli & Locatello, "λ-JEPA: Spectral Anti-Collapse Regularization for Self-Supervised Learning."** arXiv 2609.35288, v1 2026-09-28 (v2 exists; in `refs.bib`). [checked: §3.1, App. A.1.2-A.1.5]
- **Setting.** A supervised two-layer linear net ŷ = W₂W₁x with MSE loss. This is not a JEPA or stop-gradient objective.
- **Layer imbalance.** Δ = W₂ᵀW₂ − W₁W₁ᵀ; "λ-balanced" means Δ = λ_bal I.
- **Lemma A.2.** Under gradient flow on any loss ℓ(W₂W₁) without regularisation, dΔ/dt = 0. Remark A.3 notes it is exact only in continuous time.
- **Lemma A.4 (non-collapse under negative balance).** If Δ = λ_bal I with λ_bal < 0, then W₁W₁ᵀ ⪰ −λ_bal I and σ_min(W₁) ≥ √(−λ_bal). With Σ_x ⪰ κ_x I, the hidden covariance satisfies C_h ⪰ −κ_x λ_bal I.
- **Thm 3.1 / A.5.** Minimisers of (λ_reg/2)(‖W₁‖² + ‖W₂‖²) − (γ/2) log det(W₁W₁ᵀ) subject to W₂W₁ = M satisfy Δ = −(γ/λ_reg) I.
- **Thm A.7.** Under gradient flow on MSE + λ_reg-weight-decay + log-det, τΔ̇ = −2λ_reg(Δ − Δ⋆), so Δ(t) − Δ⋆ = e^{−2λ_reg t/τ}(Δ(0) − Δ⋆) with Δ⋆ = −(γ/λ_reg) I. The task-loss terms cancel. The encoder stays full row rank for all t.
- **SACReg.** A covariance log-det regulariser on view-averaged backbone features. λ-JEPA beats LeJEPA and VISReg on IN-1k and LeVJEPA and V-JEPA 2 on SSv2 and K400.

**Relation to our identity** [our derivation from their equations]:
1. **Thm A.7 with γ = 0 is our identity.** Setting γ = 0 gives Δ(t) = e^{−2λt}Δ(0), the matrix form of our p_k² − a_k² = e^{−2λt}(p0² − a0²). Take W₁ as encoder, W₂ as predictor, and project onto direction k. That projection needs the per-mode decoupling of our linear model. It holds in JEPA too, because with a stop-gradient target the online loss is ℓ(PW) at each instant, so the task terms cancel in Δ̇ in the same way. Same-rate decay needs λ_W = λ_P in gradient-flow units. With predictor learning-rate ratio α and separate decays the identity changes form, so state that assumption explicitly.
2. **Opposite sign of imbalance.** λ-JEPA's guarantee uses negative balance (encoder-heavy, Δ < 0), whose floor is a_k² ≥ −Δ_kk. Our regime is the opposite, positive balance (predictor-heavy, p0 ≫ a0, Δ > 0), where the identity gives p_k ≥ a_k and no floor on a_k. Under pure weight decay neither sign is permanent: Δ → 0, so any floor decays as e^{−2λt}. This is why collapse can still win our race, whereas their log-det term makes Δ⋆ ≠ 0 a fixed point.
3. **What the log-det term does to the race.** In our language it turns the decaying identity into a_k² ≥ γ/λ asymptotically, a permanent floor. That removes the race entirely, since no direction can collapse. λ-JEPA is therefore an anti-collapse intervention that acts directly on the quantity our race is about, but it is not a theory of when collapse happens. It does not model drive c_k, noise σ_k, gain decay or timing.
4. **Prior sources to cite with it.** The conserved and weight-decay-damped Δ predates λ-JEPA: Du-Hu-Lee 2018 (conservation), Kunin et al. 2021 Eq. 20 and Kobayashi 2024 Lemma B.1 (e^{−2λt} damping), and Dominé 2024 (λ-balanced exact solutions). Cite these for the identity, not λ-JEPA alone.

### C1. Conserved quantities and their damping by weight decay

**Kunin, Sagastuy-Brena, Ganguli, Yamins & Tanaka, "Neural Mechanics: Symmetry and Broken Conservation Laws in Deep Learning Dynamics" (ICLR 2021).** arXiv 2012.04728, v1 2020-12-08. [checked: Eqs. 19-20 in PDF]
- **Rescale symmetry under gradient flow with weight decay λ and modified loss.** |θ_A1(t)|² − |θ_A2(t)|² = e^{−2λt}(|θ_A1(0)|² − |θ_A2(0)|²) + η∫₀ᵗ e^{−2λ(t−τ)}(|g_θA1|² − |g_θA2|²)dτ.
- **Scale symmetry.** |θ_A(t)|² = e^{−2λt}|θ_A(0)|² + η∫e^{−2λ(t−τ)}|g_A|²dτ.
- **Means for us:** our balance identity is the η → 0 case of their Eq. 20. **Cite it as the origin of the damped identity.** The η-term is the finite-learning-rate correction, of order η × the gradient-norm difference. It is what makes the identity approximate in discrete training and adds a positive drift to p² − a² when predictor gradients dominate.

**Kobayashi, Akram & von Oswald 2024** (2410.23819; in `lit_tools.md`).
- Lemma B.1: AᵀA − BᵀB = Q e^{−2λt/τ}, the matrix form of the identity. Prop. B.2 covers momentum and decoupled weight decay.

**Du, Hu & Lee, "Algorithmic Regularization in Learning Deep Homogeneous Models: Layers are Automatically Balanced" (NeurIPS 2018).** arXiv 1806.00900, v1 2018-06-04.
- In linear and ReLU homogeneous nets, gradient flow conserves the differences of squared layer norms between adjacent layers, and the matrix W_{h+1}ᵀW_{h+1} − W_hW_hᵀ for linear nets.
- **Means for us:** the base invariant holds for ReLU encoders per neuron, so the balance part of the race carries over to one-hidden-layer ReLU.

**Marcotte, Gribonval & Peyré, "Abide by the Law and Follow the Flow: Conservation Laws for Gradient Flows" (NeurIPS 2023).** arXiv 2307.00144, v1 2023-06-30.
- For shallow ReLU and linear nets the known balance laws are the complete set of independent conservation laws.

**Ziyin, Li & Ueda, "Noise Balance and Stationary Distribution of Stochastic Gradient Descent."** arXiv 2308.06671, v1 2023-08-13. Phys. Rev. E per the agent.
- Under rescaling symmetry, minibatch noise drives solutions to a "noise-balanced" point.
- **Means for us:** together with Ziyin et al. 2024 (B3), SGD noise is a second, non-weight-decay force on p² − a².

### C2. Unbalanced initialisation; lazy vs rich (sign of the conserved quantity)

**Kunin, Raventós, Dominé, Chen, Klindt, Saxe & Ganguli, "Get rich quick: exact solutions reveal how unbalanced initializations promote rapid feature learning" (NeurIPS 2024).** arXiv 2406.06158, v1 2024-06-10. [checked: definition of δ]
- **Model.** f = a·wᵀx. The conserved quantity is δ = η_w a² − η_a‖w‖², i.e. last² − first² at equal learning rates. Their experiments set δ = τ²(α² − α⁻²).
- **Regimes.** δ > 0 is "upstream" (the first layer learns faster); δ < 0 is "downstream".
- **Exact solution.** With μ = a‖w‖ and φ = cos∠(w, β*):
  - μ̇ = √(δ² + 4η_aη_wμ²)(φ‖β*‖ − μ)
  - φ̇ = 2η_aη_wμ‖β*‖(1 − φ²)/(√(δ² + 4η_aη_wμ²) − δ)
- **Linear nets.** δ ≫ 0 is lazy, δ = 0 is rich, δ ≪ 0 is "delayed rich". Rapid rich learning requires balance.
- **Piecewise-linear nets.** The per-neuron δ_k is conserved, and upstream init gives an "extremely fast alignment phase", accelerating rich learning. No weight decay.
- **Means for us:**
  - **Our regime is upstream.** With equal learning rates our p0² − a0² is exactly their δ, and p0 ≫ a0 means δ > 0.
  - **Linear picture.** In their linear model upstream means the light layer moves under a nearly frozen heavy layer, which is our a' = a g(p) phase. In supervised regression this is the lazy regime. JEPA differs because the target is self-generated through the EMA or stop-gradient, so "lazy" here means slow exponential growth of a_k rather than kernel fitting.
  - **Weight decay.** It makes δ(t) = δ0 e^{−2λt}, so the system drifts from upstream toward balanced, which they do not model.
  - **ReLU.** Their result predicts fast directional alignment of encoder neurons under our init. That is a reason to expect the race to be decided early, and faster, in ReLU encoders.

**Dominé, Anguita, Proca, Braun, Kunin, Mediano & Saxe, "From Lazy to Rich: Exact Learning Dynamics in Deep Linear Networks" (ICLR 2025).** arXiv 2409.14623, v1 2024-09-22.
- **Exact dynamics.** With λ-balanced init W₂ᵀW₂ − W₁W₁ᵀ = λI conserved, they give an exact matrix-Riccati solution for QQᵀ(t), where Q stacks W₁ᵀ and W₂.
- **Aligned case.** s_α(t) = s_α(0) + γ_α(t; λ)(s̃_α − s_α(0)). It is sigmoidal (rich) as λ → 0 and 1 − e^{−|λ|t/τ} (lazy) as |λ| → ∞.
- **Weights.** S₁ = (S_λ − λ/2)^{1/2}, S₂ = (S_λ + λ/2)^{1/2}.
- Only two layers despite the title; no weight decay.
- **Means for us:** their λ is our p0² − a0², and the factor formulas are our hyperbola. A weight-decayed solution with λ(t) = λ0 e^{−2λ_wd t} is not available anywhere; deriving it would extend this line.

**Azulay, Moroshko, Nacson, Woodworth, Srebro, Globerson & Soudry, "On the Implicit Bias of Initialization Shape: Beyond Infinitesimal Mirror Descent" (ICML 2021).** arXiv 2102.09769, v1 2021-02-19.
- **Shape and scale.** δ = a² − ‖w‖² is conserved. Shape s = (|a|/‖w‖ − 1)/(|a|/‖w‖ + 1), scale α = |a|‖w‖. The implicit regulariser q_δ is given in closed form.
- **Limit.** s → 1 is the NTK regime, because "gradients of a are much smaller than gradients of w".
- **Means for us:** our init (|p| ≫ |a|) is s → 1. JEPA escapes kernel behaviour only through the self-generated target.

**Jarvis, Lee, Dominé, Saxe & Sarao Mannelli, "A Theory of Initialisation's Impact on Specialisation" (ICLR 2025).** arXiv 2503.02526, v1 2025-03-04. J. Stat. Mech. 2025 114001 per the agent.
- The conserved imbalance λ = h² − wwᵀ: "output heavy imbalance promotes feature learning" and specialisation.
- Sign conventions differ between the main text and the appendix (agent warning).

**Nam, Lee, Domine, Park, London, Choi, Goring & Lee, "Position: Solve Layerwise Linear Models First to Understand Neural Dynamical Phenomena…" (ICML 2025).** arXiv 2502.21009, v1 2025-02-28.
- A review of λ-balanced exact solutions. Large |λ|: "only the lighter layer is trained" (lazy); small |λ|: rich.

**Anguita, Locatello, Saxe, Mondelli, Mancini, Lippl & Domine, "A Theory of How Pretraining Shapes Inductive Bias in Fine-Tuning."** arXiv 2602.20062, v1 2026-02-23. ICML 2026 per the agent.
- Diagonal linear nets: a smaller init in earlier layers lets the network both reuse and refine features. There are four fine-tuning regimes set by absolute and relative scale.

**Tu, Aranguri & Jacot, "Mixed Dynamics In Linear Networks: Unifying the Lazy and Active Regimes."** arXiv 2405.17580, v1 2024-05-27.
- Each singular value is lazy below a threshold and balanced/active above it. Values start lazy, align, then become active.
- **Means for us:** a per-mode lazy-to-active threshold crossing is the supervised analogue of reaching our capture nullcline.

**Min, Tarmoun, Vidal & Mallada, "Convergence and Implicit Bias of Gradient Flow on Overparametrized Linear Networks."** arXiv 2105.06351, v1 2021-05-13.
- The convergence rate is set by the level of imbalance and the init margin.

**Chizat, Oyallon & Bach, "On Lazy Training in Differentiable Programming" (NeurIPS 2019).** arXiv 1812.07956, v1 2018-12-19.

**Woodworth, Gunasekar, Lee, Moroshko, Savarese, Golan, Soudry & Srebro, "Kernel and Rich Regimes in Overparametrized Models" (COLT 2020).** arXiv 2002.09277, v1 2020-02-20.
- Background on lazy vs rich; scale (Chizat) and init scale (Woodworth) control the transition.

**Kumar, Bordelon, Gershman & Pehlevan, "Grokking as the Transition from Lazy to Rich Training Dynamics" (ICLR 2024).** arXiv 2310.06110, v1 2023-10-09.
- An output scale controls the feature-learning rate.
- Possibly the "Kumar et al. 2024" cited by λ-JEPA for asymmetric scalings; not confirmed.

**Non-arXiv, venue as reported by the agent** [venue not independently verified unless marked]:
- **Tarmoun, Franca, Haeffele & Vidal, ICML 2021, PMLR 139:10153-10161.** Exact Riccati solution for unbalanced init. The Gramian difference is conserved, and the imbalance accelerates convergence.
- **Xu, Min, Tarmoun, Mallada & Vidal, AISTATS 2023, PMLR 206:2262-2284.** The gradient-flow imbalance speed-up does not carry over fully to fixed-step GD.
